\par\par \uc2 STOCKS &AMP; BONDS\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Markets Drift Lower Awaiting a Major U.S. Jobs Report\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Associated Press \par \uc2 675 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 June 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks drifted lower on Thursday as financial markets locked in their final moves before a highly anticipated update coming Friday about the U.S. job market.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 fell 0.5 percent for its first drop in four days. After sprinting through May and rallying within a couple good days' worth of gains of its high, the index at the center of many 401(k) accounts has lost momentum.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones industrial average dropped 108 points, or 0.3 percent, and the Nasdaq composite sank 0.8 percent.\par \par \uc2 Trading activity in options markets suggests investors believe the next big move for the S&P 500 could come on Friday, when the \uc2 U.S. Labor Department\uc2  will say how many more jobs U.S. employers created than destroyed during May. The expectation on Wall Street is for a slowdown in hiring from April.\par \par \uc2 A resilient job market has been one of the linchpins that's propped up the U.S. economy, and the worry is that all the uncertainty created by President Trump's on-and-off tariffs could push businesses to freeze their hiring.\par \par \uc2 A report on Thursday said more U.S. workers applied for unemployment benefits last week than economists expected. The number remains relatively low compared with history, but it still hit its highest level in eight months.\par \par \uc2 The data came as \uc2 Procter & Gamble\uc2 , the giant behind such brands as Pampers diapers and Cascade dish detergent, said it will cut up to 7,000 jobs over the next two years. Its stock fell 1.9 percent.\par \par \uc2 The day's heaviest weight on the market was \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , which tumbled 14.3 percent. It's lost nearly 30 percent of its value so far this year as \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's relationship with Mr. Trump sours amid a disagreement over the president's signature bill of tax cuts and spending.\par \par \uc2 Brown-Forman\uc2 , the company behind Jack Daniel's and Woodford Reserve, dropped 17.9 percent for its worst day since it began trading in 1972.\par \par \uc2 Its profit and revenue for the latest quarter fell short of Wall Street's expectations, and the company said it expects its upcoming fiscal year to be challenging because of ''consumer uncertainty, the potential impact from currently unknown tariffs'' and other things.\par \par \uc2 The chief executive of PVH, which runs the Calvin Klein and Tommy Hilfiger brands, likewise cited challenges from ''an increasingly uncertain consumer and macroeconomic backdrop.''\par \par \uc2 Its stock fell 18 percent even though it reported stronger revenue and profit for the latest quarter than analysts expected. The company cut its profit forecast for its full fiscal year, saying it will likely be able to offset only some of the potential hit it will take because of tariffs.\par \par \uc2 Hopes that Mr. Trump will lower his tariffs after reaching trade deals with other countries have been among the main reasons the S&P 500 has rallied so furiously since dropping roughly 20 percent from its record two months ago. It's now back within 3.3 percent of its high.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump stimulated such hopes Thursday after saying he had ''a very good phone call'' with China's leader, Xi Jinping, about trade and that ''their respective teams will be meeting shortly at a location to be determined.''\par \par \uc2 It's an easing of tensions after the world's two largest economies had earlier accused each other of violating the agreement that had paused their stiff tariffs against each other, which threatened to drag the economy into a recession.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, nothing is assured, and markets took the latest detente with China relatively coolly.\par \par \uc2 Among Wall Street's winners was \uc2 MongoDB\uc2 , which jumped 12.8 percent after the database company likewise delivered a stronger profit than analysts expected.\par \par \uc2 Circle Internet Group\uc2 , the U.S.-based issuer of one of the most popular cryptocurrencies, surged 168.5 percent in its first day of trading on the New York Stock Exchange.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250606el6600052\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
