\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 The Digest\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Associated Press and John Koblin \par \uc2 507 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 June 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LABOR\par \par \uc2 Disney\uc2  to Fire Hundreds\par \par \uc2 Of Employees Worldwide\par \par \uc2 The Walt Disney Co.\uc2  is laying off several hundred of its staff as it looks to trim some costs and adapt to evolving industry conditions.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Disney\uc2  spokesperson confirmed the action on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The exact number of jobs being cut is unknown, but layoffs will occur across several divisions, including television and film marketing, TV publicity, casting and development, and corporate financial operations.\par \par \uc2 No entire teams will be eliminated.\par \par \uc2 ''We have identified opportunities to operate more efficiently and are eliminating a limited number of positions,'' the spokesperson said.\par \par \uc2 Disney\uc2  posted solid profits and revenue in the second quarter as its domestic theme parks thrived and the company added well over a million subscribers to its streaming service. The company also boosted its profit expectations for the year.\par \par \uc2 Disney\uc2  has been riding a wave of box office hits, including ''Lilo & Stitch,'' which is now the second-highest grossing movie of the year, with $280.1 million in domestic ticket sales.\par \par \uc2 In 2023 the company's chief executive, \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2 , announced that \uc2 Disney\uc2  would cut about 7,000 jobs as part of an ambitious plan and ''strategic reorganization.'' \uc2 Disney\uc2  said at the time that the job reductions were part of a targeted $5.5 billion in cost savings across the company.\par \par \uc2 Shares of \uc2 Disney\uc2 , which is based in Burbank, Calif., rose slightly in midday trading.\par \par \uc2 Associated Press\par \par \uc2 ENTERTAINMENT\par \par \uc2 Popular Game Shows\par \par \uc2 Find Streaming Homes\par \par \uc2 Two of the biggest holdouts from the streaming era are finally joining the fray. New episodes of ''Jeopardy!'' and ''Wheel of Fortune'' will be available on both \uc2 Hulu\uc2  and Peacock in September, \uc2 Sony Pictures Entertainment\uc2 , the owner of the game shows, announced on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The episodes will hit the streaming services the day after they air on broadcast television, \uc2 Sony\uc2  said. This will be the first time that newer episodes of either show will be available on a streaming platform.\par \par \uc2 The deal amounts to an experiment. \uc2 Hulu\uc2  and Peacock will allow \uc2 Sony\uc2  to keep the shows on broadcast television while trying to attract a meaningful audience of younger viewers on streaming.\par \par \uc2 It also sets up the possibility for a blockbuster deal in 2028, when all of \uc2 Sony\uc2 's syndication deals expire, and exclusive rights to ''Wheel of Fortune'' and ''Jeopardy!'' will be up for grabs. Both shows could be removed from television and appear exclusively on a streaming service.\par \par \uc2 Only older episodes of ''Wheel of Fortune'' and ''Jeopardy!'' have been available on streaming services in recent years. Last year, \uc2 Sony\uc2  let a deal expire for older episodes with Pluto, a free streaming service. The \uc2 Hulu\uc2  and Peacock deals will include access to older episodes as well.\par \par \uc2 ''Wheel of Fortune'' and ''Jeopardy!'' produce a staggering volume of new episodes: 425 a year combined.\par \par \uc2 Both shows remain very popular, with each drawing roughly seven million viewers a night. But like viewership for everything else on traditional television, the audience is declining and getting older. John Koblin\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250604el640004g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
