\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Cuomo Proposes Raising New York City Minimum Wage to $20 by 2027\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emma G. Fitzsimmons \par \uc2 1,011 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 May 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Former Gov. \uc2 Andrew M. Cuomo\uc2 , the front-runner in the New York City mayor's race, announced a plan to raise the city's minimum wage to $20 an hour by 2027.\par \par \uc2 As he runs for mayor of New York City, \uc2 Andrew M. Cuomo\uc2  has made no secret of what he thinks was one of his greatest achievements as the state's governor.\par \par \uc2 ''The first state to pass the highest minimum wage in the United States of America, right here,'' he said recently.\par \par \uc2 Now Mr. Cuomo wants New York City to go further. He announced on Wednesday that, if elected, he would seek to raise the minimum wage in the five boroughs to $20 an hour by 2027, which would be among the highest in the nation.\par \par \uc2 He unveiled the new plank of his economic platform at a campaign rally in Manhattan, where he also rolled out a slate of new union endorsements. They included the Retail, Wholesale and Department Store Union, which has been fighting for a minimum-wage increase.\par \par \uc2 ''The best way to combat affordability, the best way, is to raise wages,'' Mr. Cuomo, the front-runner in the Democratic primary next month, told a room packed with union members. ''Put more money in people's pockets.''\par \par \uc2 To make the proposal reality, Mr. Cuomo would need to win support from Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  and state lawmakers, many of whom still harbor resentments from his days as governor. The state raised the minimum wage to $16.50 an hour this year in New York City, Westchester, and Long Island; it is $15.50 in the rest of the state and is expected to increase in the future based on inflation.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for the governor did not immediately respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Many states have moved to raise the minimum wage as inflation has worsened. Washington, D.C., has one of the highest minimum wages at $17.50 per hour; several communities in Washington State have a minimum wage above $20. The federal minimum wage is $7.25 per hour.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cuomo said his proposal would raise wages for roughly 800,000 workers in New York City. He is calling for the new rate to start on Jan. 1, 2027, the one-year mark of his first term if he wins.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cuomo's rivals in the mayor's race are skeptical about that commitment. They argued that he prioritized his wealthy donors and did not do enough to address affordability as governor -- a position he resigned from in 2021 after a series of sexual harassment allegations that he denies.\par \par \uc2 ''He would say anything to get elected,'' Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2 , who is running as an independent in the general election in November, said at his weekly news conference on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Many of the other Democrats who are running for mayor have announced their own plans to address affordability and income inequality, including by raising the minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 Zohran Mamdani, a progressive state lawmaker who is in second place behind Mr. Cuomo in polls, proposed raising the city's minimum wage to $30 an hour by 2030. His campaign panned Mr. Cuomo's latest proposal.\par \par \uc2 ''Cuomo is once again asking working people to lower their expectations,'' Lekha Sunder, a spokeswoman for Mr. Mamdani, said in a statement. ''There is no limit to what the disgraced ex-gov will do for his billionaire donors while telling everyone else to settle for crumbs.''\par \par \uc2 But it is Mr. Cuomo, a moderate Democrat, who has vacuumed up the support of most big labor unions in the race. His message centers on improving public safety, but he has also sought to address the city's affordability crisis, embracing some of his rivals' ideas like making free preschool for 3-year-olds universal and expanding a free bus program.\par \par \uc2 In his remarks on Wednesday, Mr. Cuomo took apparent aim at his opponents' criticism, openly taunting the city's business class.\par \par \uc2 ''The businesspeople are going to say, 'Oh no, you can't raise the minimum wage, because that's going to slow the economy,''' he said. ''Baloney!''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cuomo did not always support raising the wage so aggressively, or just for New York City. He swatted away a request for an increase by Mayor \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2  in 2014, arguing that allowing local governments to set their own rates would create ''a chaotic situation.''\par \par \uc2 Under pressure from unions and workers, Mr. Cuomo eventually had a change of heart and signed a law in 2016 to raise the wage floor to $15 for most workers, while establishing different minimums for different parts of the state. That increase put the state at the forefront of a national movement to raise wages, but New York later fell behind other states.\par \par \uc2 His campaign said that the proposed minimum wage was similar to what it would have been if the 2016 increase had been indexed to the Consumer Price Index rate for the Northeast. At a compound annual growth rate of 2.7 percent, the minimum wage would have reached $19.57 after 10 years, his campaign said.\par \par \uc2 Under Mr. Cuomo's proposal, the wage would not automatically continue to rise with the cost of living, but he called for a study to be completed by the city by Jan. 1, 2027, to inform further increases.\par \par \uc2 He also suggested creating a tax credit for small businesses to help them adjust to the higher wage. The credit would be available to businesses with 10 or fewer full-time employees and would cover up to 30 percent of the wage increase per employee. It would decrease over time.\par \par \uc2 The policy rollout was one of the few publicized events Mr. Cuomo has hosted over the last month, as he seeks to protect his polling lead by avoiding possible confrontations or missteps in public. As has become his habit, he left the event without taking questions from reporters.\par \par \uc2 Nicholas Fandos contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Nicholas Fandos contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250529el5t0004b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
