\par\par \uc2 Dining In, Dining Out / Style Desk; SECTD\par \b \uc2 Effects of 'No Tax on Tips' On Workers and Diners\b0\par\par\uc2 By Julia Moskin \par \uc2 980 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 May 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 8\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The budget bill approved by the House promises to benefit many food workers, but some details are still unclear.\par \par \uc2 As a slogan in last year's presidential campaign, ''no tax on tips'' was short, memorable and effective.\par \par \uc2 As tax policy, it is more complex.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the House passed a domestic spending bill that includes a provision to to give tipped workers a tax break on gratuities. following through on a vow made by both President Trump and former Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . The bill faces changes before it reaches Mr. Trump's desk, but the Senate has already voted unanimously for a No Tax on Tips measure.\par \par \uc2 The new bill will make the change effective from 2026 to 2028. How will it affect food-industry workers and diners? Here's what we've learned from interviews with tax lawyers, hospitality professors and industry groups:\par \par \uc2 How are restaurant employees currently taxed on tips?\par \par \uc2 The federal tax code requires that every tip be reported as income. Whether it's a $20 bill handed to a host, a line filled in on a check or a button pressed on a keypad, employees and employers are required to track and report every cent. This applies whether each worker keeps the tips or the money is pooled and redistributed.\par \par \uc2 What counts as a tip?\par \par \uc2 In the tax code and in this legislation, the term ''cash tip'' applies to tips given in bills and coins, on a credit or debit card, or via the business's electronic payment system. It has not yet been determined whether tips that go directly to a server via a service like Venmo or \uc2 PayPal\uc2  would qualify as cash. Service charges, which are legal in some places, are added by the business and do not count as tips.\par \par \uc2 What will the new bill do?\par \par \uc2 It will make tip income exempt from federal income taxes. That amount will be subtracted from reported income as an ''above the line'' deduction on a tax return. That will reduce how much income tax is owed. The tips will still have to be tracked and reported.\par \par \uc2 Who will get the deduction?\par \par \uc2 There is debate over who will benefit from the measure, which applies to all tipped workers in the restaurant business, including not only servers but also baristas, food delivery drivers and anyone holding out a payment screen after they have sold you food. According to government data, there are more than two million tipped restaurant workers in the United States.\par \par \uc2 How will this affect restaurant employees?\par \par \uc2 It will put more money in the pockets of tipped workers, like servers and bartenders, who interact directly with customers. Those employees will be able to deduct tips, unless they earn more than $160,000 in a year. (The amount will rise over time.)\par \par \uc2 Does this affect the tipped minimum wage?\par \par \uc2 No. The federal minimum wage is $7.25 per hour. (Many states and cities have higher ones.) Employers have been allowed to pay tipped workers just $2.13 of that sum since 1991. They can pay the lower wage only when the employee's tips bring the total up to minimum wage. Those tips are also subject to income tax.\par \par \uc2 Will the change result in customers being asked to tip in more places?\par \par \uc2 Not necessarily. The measure applies only to employees who are ''customarily'' tipped, to be determined by the Treasury Department. Making tips tax-free might motivate businesses to encourage tipping, in order to retain employees who are chasing this deduction. It will be unlikely to reduce tipping prompts or the growing trend of tipping on more services.\par \par \uc2 How much of my tip goes directly to the server?\par \par \uc2 That depends on the workplace and local laws. Some restaurants allow servers to keep all their tips; others require tip pooling, which shares the money with bussers, bartenders and other staff members. This bill will not change who receives tips or how they are distributed.\par \par \uc2 Does tipping in cash make a difference?\par \par \uc2 Not for tax purposes. It does reduce a restaurant's credit card processing fees, which are charged as a percentage of the total bill (about 2 percent to 3.5 percent of each transaction, and sometimes a 30-cent ''swipe'' fee). But it doesn't leave a paper trail for the employee or employer to track, which can increase the risk of tax fraud. Some cash tips go unreported, which is considered tax evasion, but the scale of the problem has never been determined. As fewer customers pay with cash, it is probably diminishing.\par \par \uc2 Will this change federal rules that prevent kitchen workers from receiving tips?\par \par \uc2 No. So-called back-of-house employees, including chefs, cooks, dishwashers and porters, cannot receive any tips unless they participate in a legal tip pool. There are federal regulations that govern tip pools, and some provisions vary state by state.\par \par \uc2 Will tips continue to count as payroll, for federal tax purposes?\par \par \uc2 Yes. In addition to withholding employee contributions for Medicare, Social Security (FICA) and unemployment, employers must pay taxes for those programs based on the employees' total wages, which include tips.\par \par \uc2 Will employers of tipped workers continue to get a tip credit?\par \par \uc2 The tip credit, officially called the 45B tax credit, helps employers of tipped employees by reducing their tax bill. They receive a credit for a portion of the Medicare and Social Security taxes they pay on tipped wages that exceed the $7.25-an-hour federal minimum wage. The new legislation will expand that credit to beauty businesses like salons and spas, but the restaurant portion will stay the same.\par \par \uc2 Follow New York Times Cooking on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  , \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 YouTube\uc2  , \uc2 TikTok\uc2  and \uc2 Pinterest\uc2  . Get regular updates from New York Times Cooking, with recipe suggestions, cooking tips and shopping advice .\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250528el5s0002y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
