\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 N.J. Transit and Union Reach Deal to End Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patrick McGeehan \par \uc2 1,073 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 May 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The agency said its trains would start running again on Tuesday morning.\par \par \uc2 An agreement was reached on Sunday to end New Jersey's first statewide transit strike in more than 40 years just three days after it started, \uc2 New Jersey Transit\uc2  and a union spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 The union that represents the state's passenger-train drivers, the \uc2 Brotherhood of Locomotive Engineers and Trainmen\uc2 , said it called off the strike at about 6 p.m., and \uc2 NJ Transit\uc2  said its trains would begin running a full schedule again on Tuesday morning.\par \par \uc2 Kris Kolluri, the chief executive of \uc2 NJ Transit\uc2 , said it would take a day to conduct safety inspections and inspect tracks before service could resume.\par \par \uc2 For Monday, the agency said, it would rely on its original strike contingency plan involving chartered buses running from four satellite locations into New York City or to stations on the PATH commuter train service.\par \par \uc2 ''The sound that you probably hear is the sound of our state's commuters breathing a collective sigh of relief, said Gov. \uc2 Philip D. Murphy\uc2 , who announced the agreement at a news conference on Sunday night.\par \par \uc2 ''If both employers and employees could please give us one more day of work from home, that would be a huge, huge boost,'' Mr. Murphy said. State officials had asked commuters to work from home during the strike if their presence in the workplace was not considered essential.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Murphy declined to provide any details of the tentative agreement but said it was ''fair to \uc2 NJ Transit\uc2 's employees while also being affordable for our state's commuters and taxpayers.''\par \par \uc2 The main sticking point had been the engineers' demand that they be paid on par with their counterparts who drive trains for other passenger railroads, including \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  and New York's commuter railroads. Mr. Kolluri implied that the agreement involved some concessions by the engineers on work rules that would help cover the cost of the raises they sought.\par \par \uc2 He did not say how much the strike had cost the agency, but he had estimated that it would cost about $4 million per day.\par \par \uc2 ''It is now the union's job to go back and brief their members and put the agreement up for ratification,'' Mr. Kolluri said.\par \par \uc2 The engineers had already voted down a previous agreement Mr. Kolluri had reached with the leaders of their union. But Mr. Murphy said he had ''a high degree of confidence'' that the engineers would find the new terms acceptable.\par \par \uc2 The engineers walked out at 12:01 a.m. on Friday, bringing New Jersey's network of commuter train lines to a halt and leaving thousands of commuters scrambling to find other ways of getting to work. \uc2 NJ Transit\uc2 , which operates the nation's third-largest commuter railroad, said it carried about 350,000 passengers per day, including about 70,000 who ride its trains into Manhattan on a typical weekday.\par \par \uc2 The engineers' union had been holding out for a new contract for more than five years and was the only one of 15 unions that represent \uc2 NJ Transit\uc2  rail employees that had not come to terms with the agency in recent years.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kolluri had implored customers to work from home during the strike if possible because the chartered buses would only be able to accommodate about one-fifth of the usual New York-bound train riders. Some large companies agreed to allow employees to work from home during the strike if their presence in the workplace was not considered essential.\par \par \uc2 But on Friday morning, commuters got a taste of how disruptive a prolonged strike might be. People arrived at train stations unaware that the trains were not running, leaving them annoyed and unsure how they would get to work.\par \par \uc2 In addition to commuter hassle, the strike had also threatened to cause economic damage to the New York area.\par \par \uc2 The Partnership for New York City, which represents large employers, said each hour that \uc2 New Jersey Transit\uc2 's rail commuters were delayed in getting to work reduced overall productivity in the city by $6 million.\par \par \uc2 State officials and union leaders blamed each other for the breakdown in talks on Thursday night that spurred the strike.\par \par \uc2 Governor Murphy castigated the union, saying, ''It is frankly a mess of their own making and it is a slap in the face of every commuter and worker who relies on \uc2 NJ Transit\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 The union's general chairman, Thomas Haas, and Mr. Kolluri announced in March that they had reached a tentative agreement that would head off a strike. Mr. Kolluri said that deal would have raised the engineers' average annual pay to $172,000, from $135,000.\par \par \uc2 But the engineers overwhelmingly rejected the terms of that agreement in April, reviving the threat of a walkout. Mr. Haas said the agency's representatives had walked away from the bargaining table on Thursday night, leaving his members no alternative but to leave \uc2 NJ Transit\uc2  with nobody to drive its trains.\par \par \uc2 The two sides met for much of the weekend to try to resolve their differences before feeling the accumulating frustration of dislocated commuters.\par \par \uc2 After holding an abruptly scheduled meeting on Saturday afternoon, they met again on Sunday in a session mediated by the \uc2 National Mediation Board\uc2 , which had been involved in the talks since Monday.\par \par \uc2 Jay Frederick, 47, who runs the commissary at Northern State Prison in Newark, said he spent Friday at home because he was not able to get to work.\par \par \uc2 ''It's a relief,'' that the strike is over., Mr. Frederick said. ''I like commuting. The hustle and bustle is good for me. But not this much hustle and bustle. You need these things, these trains and buses, to be in place.''\par \par \uc2 Chris Smith, 54, said he paid $79 for a one-way ticket on \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  to get to his job as an executive chef in Midtown Manhattan on Friday. He said he was ready to book himself a hotel room in the city this week if the strike continued.\par \par \uc2 He was not impressed by the speed with which \uc2 NJ Transit\uc2  struck its deal. ''I think they're selfish,'' he said. ''They put tens of thousands of people in a stress environment. Friday was horrible. Trust me.''\par \par \uc2 Mark Bonamo and Tammy LaGorce contributed reporting from New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 Mark Bonamo and Tammy LaGorce contributed reporting from New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250519el5j00045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
