\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 In New Jersey, Transit Strikers Roil Commute\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patrick McGeehan \par \uc2 1,078 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The first statewide transit strike in New Jersey in more than 40 years left a fleet of passenger trains parked in rail yards and thousands of commuters stranded, annoyed and bewildered Friday morning.\par \par \uc2 The strike followed months of negotiations between \uc2 New Jersey Transit\uc2 , the nation's third-largest commuter rail network, and the union that represents train drivers, the \uc2 Brotherhood of Locomotive Engineers and Trainmen\uc2 . Union members began picketing at stations on Friday after contract talks broke down late Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 State officials said they hoped to resume negotiations with the union over the weekend so that the strike would end before Monday.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Philip D. Murphy\uc2  apologized to commuters whose trips had been disrupted and blamed union leaders for refusing to accept an offer that he said was fair to their members and fiscally responsible for the state.\par \par \uc2 ''It is frankly a mess of their own making and it is a slap in the face of every commuter and worker who relies on \uc2 NJ Transit\uc2 ,'' Mr. Murphy, a Democrat, said at a news conference in Aberdeen, N.J.\par \par \uc2 Thomas Haas, the union's general chairman, said his negotiating team had been optimistic about reaching an agreement until late Thursday.\par \par \uc2 ''They decided to walk away,'' Mr. Haas said. ''It's definitely frustrating, but we're willing to go back to the table.''\par \par \uc2 Some commuters did not learn about the shutdown until they arrived at their local train stations early Friday.\par \par \uc2 Leslie Bell, 34, was stuck at the station in Trenton, trying to get to his job as supervisor at a Wawa store in Newark. He had bought \uc2 NJ Transit\uc2  tickets in advance and his only option for getting to work on time for his 8 a.m. shift was an \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  train that would cost $110.\par \par \uc2 ''I can't pay $110 to get to Newark on \uc2 Amtrak\uc2 , plus all the tickets are sold out,'' Mr. Bell said. ''This is ridiculous.''\par \par \uc2 NJ Transit\uc2 's contingency plan called for providing chartered buses from four spots in the state as substitutes for the idle trains. But the buses could only accommodate about one-fifth of the displaced train riders, and they were not scheduled to start running until Monday.\par \par \uc2 On a typical weekday, about 70,000 commuters ride \uc2 NJ Transit\uc2  trains into New York City. But Kris Kolluri, the agency's chief executive, said the shutdown of the train lines would affect all 350,000 NJ Transit riders, including those who take buses and light rail.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kolluri, who has been leading management's bargaining team, said on Friday that he believed the two sides were 95 percent of the way to a deal. The last major sticking point has been a union demand that \uc2 NJ Transit\uc2  engineers be paid on par with their counterparts at the other passenger railroads that serve New York City, \uc2 Amtrak\uc2 , the \uc2 Long Island Rail Road\uc2  and Metro-North Railroad.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Haas said his members earned $10 an hour less than engineers at the other railroads. \uc2 NJ Transit\uc2 's offer, the union said, amounted to annual increases of about 2.7 percent over 7.5 years, compared with average annual raises of 4.87 percent it recently negotiated with \uc2 Amtrak\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kolluri said \uc2 NJ Transit\uc2  had offered the engineers a contract that would have brought their starting hourly wage to nearly $50, close to what the L.I.R.R. pays. He said a tentative contract that union members overwhelmingly rejected last month would have raised their average annual pay to $172,000 from $135,000.\par \par \uc2 Union officials disputed those figures, saying that the engineers' annual base pay was only $89,000 and that most earned less than $100,000 a year.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kolluri, who called the striking engineers his ''colleagues,'' said he expected talks to resume on Sunday, if not sooner.\par \par \uc2 While Mr. Kolluri and Mr. Murphy spoke to reporters in Aberdeen, a half-dozen striking engineers stood across the street holding signs. They said they loved their jobs and wanted to get back to work as soon as a deal could be reached.\par \par \uc2 ''I feel bad for the people we move every day, the worker bees and the sports fans,'' said Michael Delatore, 37, who drove a Coast Line train on its last run to Long Branch Thursday night. He said he was anxious and ''stressed'' about being on strike and was willing to return to work ''immediately.''\par \par \uc2 By taking the drastic action of walking out, the engineers probably gained the upper hand in the bargaining, at least temporarily, Bill Dwyer, an associate teaching professor at the Rutgers School of Management and Labor Relations, said in an interview on Friday.\par \par \uc2 ''If there's a power imbalance, I think it goes in favor of the union at this point,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Shutting down the railroad ''gives the union additional leverage because the ridership is not going to be happy,'' Professor Dwyer said. The transit agency's customers, he added, are ''going to be very impatient'' about having their service restored.\par \par \uc2 Still, he said, that advantage may be short-lived if \uc2 New Jersey Transit\uc2  retaliates by cutting off payments for the engineers' health insurance. Striking workers would start to feel the financial pinch of not getting paid after a couple of weeks, he said. Having to cover their own health-care costs could compound that discomfort quickly.\par \par \uc2 ''They are escalating the situation,'' Professor Dwyer said, referring to the union, ''and the bigger guns might start coming out.''\par \par \uc2 Matt Stratton, 47, a banker from Glen Ridge, N.J., who takes a train into Manhattan regularly, said he dreaded what would happen if the strike continued into next week.\par \par \uc2 ''I have no idea how I'm getting to work on Monday,'' he said while standing on a platform at Pennsylvania Station in Newark. ''I'm actively looking into figuring out what to do.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Stratton expressed no sympathy for either side in the dispute.\par \par \uc2 ''We just had a fare increase, and reliability is terrible,'' he said. ''I was delayed every day last week. It's hard to be sympathetic just based on the poor service.''\par \par \uc2 Tammy LaGorce, Mark Bonamo, Nate Schweber and Naim Ali-Pacheco contributed reporting from New Jersey. Cameron Oakes and Hilary Howard contributed reporting from New York.\par \par \uc2 Tammy LaGorce, Mark Bonamo, Nate Schweber and Naim Ali-Pacheco contributed reporting from New Jersey. Cameron Oakes and Hilary Howard contributed reporting from New York.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250517el5h0004k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
