\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Burberry\uc2  to Lay Off 1,700 As Luxury Sales Sag\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Paton \par \uc2 497 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 May 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 National\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The British fashion house, which reported poor annual sales in a precarious time for the global luxury industry, is looking to save 60 million pounds in cost-cutting efforts.\par \par \uc2 Burberry\uc2 , the British fashion brand known for its trench coats and signature checkered print, said on Wednesday that it would slash up to 1,700 jobs in a bid to cut costs as it tries to return to profit after a period of poor sales in an increasingly volatile luxury sector.\par \par \uc2 The job cuts, which represent 18 percent of the company's global work force, are part of larger organizational changes that are expected to save the company 60 million pounds (about $80 million). The measures come amid global uncertainty that increased after President Trump imposed tariffs on America's top trading partners.\par \par \uc2 ''The current macroeconomic environment has become more uncertain in light of geopolitical developments,'' the company said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The company announced the cost-cutting strategy after reporting that it swung to an annual loss in the 2025 financial year, which ended March 29, from a profit the year before. Operating losses were \u163\'00\'A33 million, or $4 million, down from a \u163\'00\'A3418 million, or $558 million, profit the year prior. Revenue was down 17 percent to \u163\'00\'A32.5 billion, or $3.4 billion.\par \par \uc2 Burberry\uc2  has struggled in recent years after managerial and designer changes, a drive to increase prices that fell flat with customers and the wider global slowdown of luxury consumption, particularly in China, its biggest overseas market.\par \par \uc2 The company said the cost-cutting efforts, combined with earlier measures, could lead to planned savings of up to \u163\'00\'A3100 million, or $133 million, by 2027. Most job losses will hit corporate offices but could also affect retail stores and factories.\par \par \uc2 Shares jumped by as much as 10 percent on the news after the market opened in London.\par \par \uc2 The company's chief executive, Joshua Shulman, joined \uc2 Burberry\uc2  from its American rival, Coach, last year to lead a turnaround effort. He said in a statement on Wednesday that he was ''more optimistic than ever that \uc2 Burberry\uc2 's best days are ahead and that we will deliver sustainable profitable growth over time,'' but added that the last financial year had been challenging.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Huggins, a manager at the investment firm Wealth Club, noted that a 6 percent decline in same-store sales in the fourth quarter was ''a marked improvement'' and suggested that ''the strategic plan to reignite the brand may be gaining early traction.''\par \par \uc2 ''But fiscal year 2025 was still an annus horribilis for \uc2 Burberry\uc2 ,'' he said. ''Almost everything that could go wrong did. Luxury consumers across the globe significantly tightened their belts hitting the whole luxury sector. But \uc2 Burberry\uc2  has seen more impact than most.''\par \par \uc2 Other groups that noted earnings declines recently include \uc2 LVMH\uc2 , owner of Louis Vuitton and Dior, and Kering, which owns brands like Gucci and Saint Laurent.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250515el5f00062\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
