\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Pinterest\uc2  Settles Lawsuit With an Early Adviser\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eli Tan \par \uc2 479 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 May 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Christine Martinez, who was a friend of two of \uc2 Pinterest\uc2 's three co-founders, sued the company in 2021 for breach of implied contract and other claims.\par \par \uc2 Pinterest\uc2  recently agreed to pay $34.7 million to settle a lawsuit from an early adviser who claimed she had co-created the platform without compensation.\par \par \uc2 Christine Martinez, 44, who was a friend of \uc2 Ben Silbermann\uc2  and \uc2 Paul Sciarra\uc2 , two of \uc2 Pinterest\uc2 's three co-founders, sued the company in 2021 for breach of implied contract, idea theft, unjust enrichment and unfair business practices. She said she came up with many ideas for the app -- like organizing images on ''boards'' -- but was never paid for her contributions, despite promises she would be.\par \par \uc2 Pinterest\uc2 , a virtual pinboard company that has many female users, disclosed the settlement with Ms. Martinez in a November 2024 financial filing.\par \par \uc2 ''No one wants to find themselves in the litigation process, and I'm just really, really excited and frankly just relieved to be past it,'' Ms. Martinez said in an interview on Friday.\par \par \uc2 ''Ms. Martinez provided beneficial marketing and community growth input and strategies during the early phase of \uc2 Pinterest\uc2 's founding,'' according to a statement that was part of the settlement, which was provided by Ms. Martinez. ''The parties are pleased to amicably resolve this legacy matter.''\par \par \uc2 Pinterest\uc2  declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 The settlement follows a series of complaints and legal disputes against \uc2 Pinterest\uc2  by some of its female employees and executives.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, \uc2 Pinterest\uc2  paid $22.5 million to settle a gender discrimination suit filed by \uc2 Fran\u231\'00\'E7oise Brougher\uc2 , its former chief operating officer, who said she was fired after experiencing sexist treatment at the company. That same year, more than 200 employees signed a petition demanding the company change its policies after three former workers accused \uc2 Pinterest\uc2  of racial and sex discrimination and retaliation.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Silbermann, who was \uc2 Pinterest\uc2 's chief executive, left that role in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Martinez, who had a background in e-commerce and interior design, claimed in her lawsuit that Mr. Silbermann and Mr. Sciarra sought her advice for the company that became \uc2 Pinterest\uc2  a year before it was founded in 2010.\par \par \uc2 She said she came up with the idea for the picture boards and the platform's signature ''Pin it'' phrase, and also helped persuade top design and lifestyle bloggers to use and promote the site. A portion of \uc2 Pinterest\uc2 's programming code was named after her in homage, according to the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 She never signed a formal contract with \uc2 Pinterest\uc2 , but it was implied she would eventually be compensated, she said. \uc2 Pinterest\uc2  went public in 2019 and has a market capitalization of more than $18 billion.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Martinez is now a board member and strategic adviser for Jingo, an online A.I. shopping platform that caters to women.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250510el5a00045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
