\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 As Strike Looms, N.J. Transit Riders Are Warned of 'Chaos'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Patrick McGeehan \par \uc2 950 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 May 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 21\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Tens of thousands of New Jersey commuters are being told that they may need to work from home if train engineers go on strike.\par \par \uc2 Just as the post-pandemic return to offices in New York City has achieved sustained momentum, tens of thousands of commuters from New Jersey are being warned that they may need to work from home if train engineers go on strike in mid-May.\par \par \uc2 Amid strained negotiations with the union that represents its train drivers, \uc2 New Jersey Transit\uc2  began telling its customers on Wednesday to prepare for a shutdown of the statewide rail service as soon as May 16. It would be the first such strike in New Jersey in more than 40 years.\par \par \uc2 The agency laid out a contingency plan that involves substituting buses for some of the trains that carry 70,000 commuters into Manhattan on a typical weekday. But Kris Kolluri, the new chief executive of \uc2 New Jersey Transit\uc2 , said that supplemental bus service, from four ''park and ride'' lots around the state, could carry only about 20 percent of the daily train riders.\par \par \uc2 ''Buses can never satisfy the demand we have on trains,'' Mr. Kolluri said, adding that each bus holds about 100 passengers, compared with 1,000 on an average train. ''Those folks who have the ability to work from home during the strike should plan to do so.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kolluri, who took over the agency in January, has been trying for weeks to work out a new contract with the \uc2 Brotherhood of Locomotive Engineers and Trainmen\uc2 , which represents about 460 \uc2 New Jersey Transit\uc2  engineers. Last month, the two sides announced that they had reached a deal, but in mid-April the rank-and-file engineers overwhelmingly rejected the terms.\par \par \uc2 That rejection started a 30-day countdown to a May 16 strike deadline. Beginning on that date, a Friday, the agency advised its customers to work from home if they can and to limit their use of the rail system to ''essential purposes only.''\par \par \uc2 The contingency plan would go into effect on the following Monday, May 19, Mr. Kolluri said. He said that announcing the plan was not a negotiating tactic.\par \par \uc2 ''We have an obligation to tell our riders what is about to happen on the system,'' he said. ''Two weeks is not a long time for people to start thinking about how they're going to deal with this.''\par \par \uc2 N.J. Transit said that a shutdown of the rail system would ''create the potential for overcrowding'' on its bus and light rail systems and would significantly increase congestion on the state's highways. Commuters who opt to drive into Manhattan in the event of a rail strike would face the city's new congestion pricing toll, which charges most drivers $9 to enter the zone below 60th Street.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kolluri said that Congress could intervene to prevent or stop a strike under the federal Railway Labor Act, whose purpose is to protect interstate commerce. But the impasse has already been mediated by two Presidential Emergency Boards appointed last year by \uc2 Joseph R. Biden Jr.\par \par \uc2 Negotiations resumed on Wednesday morning and are expected to continue next week, but Mr. Kolluri did not sound hopeful, referring to the ''chaos the union is intent on creating.''\par \par \uc2 Tom Haas, the union's general chairman, said in an interview that the union did not want to go on strike but could not accept a contract that left its members underpaid compared with their peers at other passenger railroads. He said the union would present a counteroffer to N.J. Transit on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Last week, Mr. Kolluri rescinded the agency's previous offer, which he said would have provided annual salaries of $172,000 on average, up from about $135,000 now. ''The union voted down an agreement that was negotiated in good faith,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kolluri said that the engineers' demand of a 14 percent wage increase would cost taxpayers and N.J. Transit $1.363 billion between July 2025 and June 2030. ''That's $684 million more than the contract proposal union members voted down on April 15,'' the agency said.\par \par \uc2 In a fact sheet posted on its website, the agency said that acceding to the union's demands would require either ''drastic reductions to service systemwide,'' a fare increase of 17 percent or a 27 percent increase in the Corporate Transit Fee, a tax on businesses that the state legislature approved last year to bridge a large gap in the agency's budget. N.J. Transit raised fares 15 percent systemwide last year to help fill that gap.\par \par \uc2 Union officials said that those estimates were wildly inflated and that the contract they were seeking would cost the agency only about $250 million.\par \par \uc2 ''Our members are angry,'' Mr. Haas said after his members voted down the agreement reached in March. ''I, along with other N.J. Transit engineers, have kept the trains moving, but we have gone without a raise since 2019, during a period of high inflation and throughout the pandemic that claimed some of our co-workers.''\par \par \uc2 The engineers' union is the only one of 15 unions that represent N.J. Transit workers that did not accept the agency's proposal for a contract that ran through June 2024. That agreement provided a total wage increase of 12 percent over four years.\par \par \uc2 Most of N.J. Transit's unions also agreed to a contract that offered 3 percent annual raises through June 2027. The agreement the engineers' union had reached last month included a 4 percent raise for the year starting July 1, 2027.\par \par 