\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 A Door Shuts for Workers Fighting Firings\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eileen Sullivan \par \uc2 1,030 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 April 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Experts in federal employment law said the Trump administration's justifications to end the investigations were baffling at best.\par \par \uc2 The independent government agency charged with protecting federal workers' rights will drop its inquiry into the more than 2,000 complaints that the Trump administration had improperly fired probationary employees, according to emailed notices received by five workers and reviewed by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The agency, the \uc2 Office of Special Counsel\uc2 , told affected employees that it had concluded that it could not pursue the claims of unlawful termination in part because they were fired not for individual cause, but en masse as part of President Trump's ''governmentwide effort to reduce the federal service.''\par \par \uc2 The decision effectively eliminates one of the few avenues government employees had to challenge their terminations. It comes as Mr. Trump has forced out the office's leader and replaced him for now with Jamieson Greer, who is also serving as U.S. trade representative.\par \par \uc2 The office is charged with protecting whistle-blowers from retaliation, which is the reason for its independent status and a Senate-confirmed leader. But it also scrutinizes other employment-related issues, including investigations into claims of prohibited personnel practices, or PPPs, such as discrimination, nepotism or an attempt to coerce political activity.\par \par \uc2 Reached for comment, the \uc2 Office of Special Counsel\uc2  declined to say how many of the more than 2,000 fired probationary employees with pending complaints actually received the notice.\par \par \uc2 Experts in federal employment law said the justifications to end the investigations were baffling at best.\par \par \uc2 Nick Bednar, an administrative law expert at the \uc2 University of Minnesota\uc2 , said that on one hand, the office argues that it is not moving forward with investigations into whether these probationary employees were removed unlawfully for poor performance because they were fired as a class and not as individuals.\par \par \uc2 But, he pointed out, the message also said the mass firings were not part of an official government reorganization process known as a reduction in force, which allows for mass firings.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bednar said the office appeared to be trying to claim that the firings were done legally another way.\par \par \uc2 ''And I do not know what that third unknown way is,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 The president, sweeping aside many of the government's internal watchdogs in the days after he took office, had moved quickly to fire the office's leader, Hampton Dellinger. Mr. Dellinger challenged his termination, and was temporarily reinstated by a lower court in February -- just long enough to begin investigating the avalanche of complaints his office had received about the mass firings of probationary workers.\par \par \uc2 At the time, he said the decision to fire probationary employees en masse ''without individualized cause'' appeared ''contrary to a reasonable reading of the law.''\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Dellinger suffered a legal setback in his own employment case, and stopped fighting his removal. Mr. Trump initially replaced him in an acting capacity with \uc2 Doug Collins\uc2 , the secretary of veterans affairs, and then replaced Mr. Collins with Mr. Greer, also in an acting capacity.\par \par \uc2 One probationary employee who was fired from \uc2 the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency\uc2 , an agency in the Treasury Department, said this outcome was expected as soon as Mr. Trump installed Mr. Collins.\par \par \uc2 That employee and others who received the email on Monday spoke with \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  on condition of anonymity out of fear of retribution, as they pursue other challenges to the administration's first major cut to the federal work force.\par \par \uc2 Employees from at least four agencies who had pursued claims were notified of the decision in emails that contained nearly identical language, according to messages shared with The Times.\par \par \uc2 They were among the thousands of employees new to their positions who were fired en masse in February for nonspecific performance issues, even as even as many had received positive reviews from their managers. Federal agencies used a template provided by the Office of Personnel Management, the government's human resources arm, to fire their employees.\par \par \uc2 Several challenges to the firings are making their way through two separate federal courts and another administrative body charged with considering appeals to personnel actions, the \uc2 Merit Systems Protection Board\uc2 . The court challenges have led to the reinstatement of thousands of other fired probationary employees, but those decisions were later overturned in appeals, leaving the workers in limbo.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration targeted probationary employees for its first major round of job cuts because they have fewer protections than workers who have been in their positions longer.\par \par \uc2 The email from the \uc2 Office of Special Counsel\uc2  said the watchdog ''is unable to pursue a claim that your probationary termination'' was a prohibited personnel practice. Even if the office could prove the termination was indeed a violation of personnel practices, it cannot investigate whether it was unlawful, the message said.\par \par \uc2 ''This is because your termination, in the context of the governmentwide effort to reduce the federal service through probationary terminations, was more likely effected in accordance with the new administration's priorities than a decision personal to you,'' it said.\par \par \uc2 Now, the employees' only avenue to challenge their firings may be one of several class-action lawsuits before the \uc2 Merit Systems Protection Board\uc2 . Government lawyers have argued that fired employees cannot bring challenges in Federal District Court because Congress established such independent administrative bodies to handle such matters.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Trump also fired the head of the merit systems board, leaving it without a quorum and unable to issue decisions. That leader, Cathy Harris, is fighting her removal, as well, in a case that has gone to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 With the latest development, one employee with the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  who was among the probationary workers fired in February said everything remained unresolved. For now, she is on administrative leave and receiving a government paycheck. But she said it was not clear how long that would last. She said she planned to fight her firing as long as she could, but that the avenues to do so were closing.\par \par \uc2 Aishvarya Kavi and Madeleine Ngo contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Aishvarya Kavi and Madeleine Ngo contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250424el4o00030\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
