\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Judge Blocks Trump's Attempt To Dismantle Voice of America\b0\par\par\uc2 By Minho Kim \par \uc2 936 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 April 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The judge also ordered the government to halt its efforts to shut down two other government-funded outlets, \uc2 Radio Free Asia\uc2  and the Middle East Broadcasting Networks.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge on Tuesday blocked the Trump administration from dismantling Voice of America, a government-funded news organization that President Trump has accused of being biased against him, and mandated that its journalists be allowed to resume their work.\par \par \uc2 The judge, Royce C. Lamberth of the U.S. District Court for the District of Columbia, also ordered the administration to halt its effort to shut down two other federally funded outlets, \uc2 Radio Free Asia\uc2  and the Middle East Broadcasting Networks.\par \par \uc2 The judge's carefully worded decision appeared aimed at closing loopholes in previous court rulings that allowed Trump officials to keep the Voice of America newsroom shuttered and its programming on hold. Voice of America, founded in 1942, had operated without interruption until March 15, a day after Mr. Trump signed an executive order seeking to gut its parent organization, the U.S. Agency for Global Media.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump has attacked Voice of America as ''the voice of radical America,'' and accused the outlet, which delivers news to countries with limited press freedom -- including Russia, China and Iran -- of spreading ''anti-American'' and partisan ''propaganda.''\par \par \uc2 Kari Lake, a close ally of Mr. Trump whom he hired as special adviser to the media agency, declared the news outlet ''unsalvageable.'' She also claimed the media agency and its newsrooms were rampant with ''waste, fraud and abuse,'' without providing details on how taxpayer money was being misused.\par \par \uc2 The executive order dismantling the outlet's parent agency appeared to test the bounds of Mr. Trump's authority, as Congress established it as independent and moved in 2020 to limit the power of its presidentially appointed leadership. Judge Lamberth is overseeing multiple court challenges to Mr. Trump's order brought by Voice of America journalists and organizations that promote press freedoms abroad.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration is ''likely in direct violation of numerous federal laws,'' Judge Lamberth, an appointee of President \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , wrote in his ruling.\par \par \uc2 As a result of the executive order, all of Voice of America's staff members were placed on paid leave, abruptly halting news programming in 49 languages that more than 425 million listeners across the world tuned into every week. Ms. Lake also shut down its radio transmitters, dismissed vendors and terminated wire services and other contracts.\par \par \uc2 Trump officials also froze funding for a number of other outlets overseen by the agency, imperiling their operations: \uc2 Radio Free Asia\uc2 , the Middle East Broadcasting Networks, and \uc2 Radio Free Europe/Radio Liberty\uc2 . Unlike Voice of America, which is a government entity, those news outlets are private nonprofits and have independent hiring authorities, but they have had to furlough their staff in recent weeks and have been forced to reduce their programming.\par \par \uc2 Judge Lamberth wrote in his ruling that in attempting to throttle the outlets in part by cutting funding and removing staff, the Trump administration had likely violated the Constitution, along with laws that govern how federal funds are spent. He also cited the International Broadcasting Act, which mandates continued operations of the media networks.\par \par \uc2 ''The president does not have unilateral authority to refuse to spend the funds,'' Judge Lamberth wrote. ''The defendants' unwillingness to expend funds in accordance with the congressional appropriations laws is a direct affront to the power of the legislative branch.''\par \par \uc2 He added: ''Congress possesses the 'power of the purse.'''\par \par \uc2 Michael Abramowitz, the director of Voice of America, said the judge's ruling ''clears the way'' for 1,300 of the agency's staff members to ''resume their critical role of providing fact-based news about America and its policies.''\par \par \uc2 Radio Free Europe/Radio Liberty\uc2 , another private nonprofit that filed a lawsuit and receives congressionally approved funding, did not get a similar relief on Tuesday despite a continued funding freeze.\par \par \uc2 In March, after Judge Lamberth prohibited the administration from terminating the grant for \uc2 Radio Free Europe/Radio Liberty\uc2 , the Trump administration reversed the termination but kept withholding the money, asserting that it was negotiating new terms of the grant agreement with the news organization.\par \par \uc2 According to court filings, Trump officials wanted to be able to unilaterally pause funds for the federally funded broadcaster and shut down parts of its programming, moves that \uc2 Radio Free Europe\uc2  argued were forbidden by Congress to ensure journalistic integrity.\par \par \uc2 Judge Lamberth on Tuesday found that it was ''premature'' for him to intervene, since the government and \uc2 Radio Free Europe\uc2  are still negotiating. But he noted that a court intervention in favor of the news group would soon be warranted, calling the negotiation ''broken down'' and the situation ''begging for'' judicial review.\par \par \uc2 During a hearing involving a case brought by Voice of America journalists last week, Judge Lamberth had zeroed in on the issue of executive authority.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers for the government defended the administration's inaction by claiming that the pause had been done to find ways to roll out Mr. Trump's directive, without entirely dismantling Voice of America. They also argued that because the news outlet's staff was on leave, rather than fired, it was not yet defying congressional mandates.\par \par \uc2 Judge Lamberth did not find the government's argument convincing. He also noted that Mr. Trump signed a budget bill that funded federal news organizations the day after he issued the executive order on those media outlets.\par \par \uc2 ''It's all a little hard to swallow,'' he said. ''The president signed the law.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250424el4n00006\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
