\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Google\uc2  Allowing Workers To Discuss Antitrust Case\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nico Grant \par \uc2 666 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 April 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Silicon Valley giant had ordered employees to stay silent on the landmark case before reaching a settlement with a workers' union over the issue.\par \par \uc2 When \uc2 Google\uc2  lost its landmark antitrust trial in August, \uc2 Kent Walker\uc2 , its top lawyer, reminded employees, for the third time, that they were not allowed to discuss the case with one another or anyone outside the company.\par \par \uc2 On Friday afternoon, \uc2 Google\uc2  rescinded the command as part of a settlement with the Alphabet Workers Union, a group representing some of its employees and contractors, according to an email that \uc2 Google\uc2  sent to workers and that was viewed by The New York Times. \uc2 Alphabet\uc2  is the parent company of \uc2 Google\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The company told employees that it would not ''announce or maintain overbroad rules or policies that restrict your right to comment, internally or externally,'' about how the antitrust lawsuit targeting \uc2 Google\uc2 's search engine might affect the terms and conditions of their employment.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2 's change of tune was part of a settlement overseen by the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 . The union had filed an unfair labor practices complaint with the N.L.R.B. concerning Mr. Walker's note in August.\par \par \uc2 The agreement is another blow to \uc2 Google\uc2 's corporate policies designed to maintain secrecy, which have been scrutinized amid the search case brought by the Justice Department. It also undercut \uc2 Google\uc2 's strategy to keep its business humming during the lawsuit -- to have employees ignore the antitrust battle and remain focused on their work.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Walker, \uc2 Google\uc2 's president of global affairs, first told employees not to discuss the case when it was filed in October 2020, according to an email viewed by The Times.\par \par \uc2 ''It's important not to get distracted by this process, including not speculating on legal issues internally or externally,'' he wrote, instead directing employees to focus on building great products and services to help people.\par \par \uc2 He repeated the call in September 2023, when the case went to trial, and again in August, when \uc2 Google\uc2  lost that trial. The N.L.R.B. settlement concerned only that final note.\par \par \uc2 Two months later, Lee-Anne Mulholland, \uc2 Google\uc2 's vice president of regulatory affairs, tried to clarify that Mr. Walker's instructions referred to speaking on \uc2 Google\uc2 's behalf to the public.\par \par \uc2 ''That was the basis of Kent's earlier request that Googlers refrain from commenting on'' the case, she said.\par \par \uc2 Courtenay Mencini, a \uc2 Google\uc2  spokeswoman, said the company disagreed with the N.L.R.B.'s interpretation of its ''reasonable request that employees not comment on a pending legal case on behalf of \uc2 Google\uc2  without approval.''\par \par \uc2 ''To avoid lengthy litigation, we agreed to remind employees that they have the right to talk about their employment, as they've always been free to and regularly do,'' she added.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department has called for a breakup of \uc2 Google\uc2 , including a divestment of Chrome, its popular web browser, among other remedies meant to restore competition in search. Amit P. Mehta, a federal judge, will decide what remedies to impose by August.\par \par \uc2 It has been hard to tune all of the legal machinations out, Stephen McMurtry, a senior software engineer at \uc2 Google\uc2  search and a member of the union, said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 ''Amongst employees generally, there is fear of instability that the breakups could provide to our employment, working conditions, compensation, all sorts of things,'' Mr. McMurtry said.\par \par \uc2 Like other large tech companies, \uc2 Google\uc2  has established a pattern of secrecy in its culture and corporate communications. After \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's legal defense was hamstrung by damaging emails during its antitrust trial a quarter century ago, \uc2 Google\uc2  tried to learn from the company's example by telling employees not to say anything that might make the company's conduct sound anticompetitive.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  also routinely discussed sensitive business matters in instant messages that were automatically deleted, though the company said it had handed over many chats to the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250409el4900035\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
