\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Surviving the Eaton Fire, Only to Be Facing Layoffs\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jesus Jim\u233\'00\'E9nez \par \uc2 1,049 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 April 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A dozen teachers in Pasadena, Calif., whose homes burned were told they'll lose their jobs at the end of the school year, a crisis for educators and students alike.\par \par \uc2 The morning after the Eaton fire broke out in Southern California, Sergio Lopez and his father returned to Altadena in a last-ditch effort to save their property. As powerful winds sent embers flying across their neighborhood, the father and son tried to douse the property with a garden hose.\par \par \uc2 But as the fire crept closer, they had no choice but to flee. The house was destroyed.\par \par \uc2 Not long after, more bad news came. Mr. Lopez, a teacher at John Muir High School in Pasadena, Calif., was at an academic conference in Sacramento when he got a phone call from the principal. He was being laid off.\par \par \uc2 Seven weeks after losing his home, he was told he was losing his job.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Lopez is one of 151 workers with the \uc2 Pasadena Unified School District\uc2  who received layoff notices this year. And he's one of a dozen teachers who both lost their homes during the Eaton fire and are preparing to lose their jobs when the school year ends.\par \par \uc2 ''After losing my home, my classroom was basically all I owned,'' Mr. Lopez said. ''Everything that I had owned after the fire was left inside my classroom.''\par \par \uc2 Layoff notices across California school districts are typical early in the calendar year. But in a school district where thousands of students and employees were forced to evacuate during the wildfire, the layoff notices in Pasadena have been a heavy blow, a crisis upon crisis for many families.\par \par \uc2 Some students who lost their homes will now lose their favorite teachers. Some teachers who've been displaced for months are now losing a colleague. Some administrators who have struggled to forge a sense of unity and normalcy are now dealing with more tension and uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 At a school board meeting in late March, many students spoke during the public comment period to show support for their teachers, pleading with district officials to reconsider the layoffs. Some students brought signs reading ''Save Our Teachers'' and ''Students Deserve Stability.''\par \par \uc2 One sophomore at Pasadena High School described a math teacher as not just an educator but a ''mentor and guide.'' Another student at James Madison Elementary School said her family had been affected by the fires and that the layoffs were creating more stress for students.\par \par \uc2 ''I just want to say this,'' she said. ''This is a very poor choice.''\par \par \uc2 When Mr. Lopez, 27, was back in front of his students after receiving the layoff notice, he planned to power through the lesson without mentioning it.\par \par \uc2 ''I saw them, and I just couldn't do it,'' Mr. Lopez said. ''I just broke down, and I told them the news. They were disappointed. They were upset.''\par \par \uc2 While much of \uc2 the city of Pasadena\uc2  emerged unscathed from the Eaton fire, many students, teachers and employees in the school district lived in nearby Altadena, which bore the brunt of the fire. Ninety teachers within the district lost their homes, according to the United Teachers of Pasadena, the union that represents the teachers.\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for the Pasadena school district did not respond to multiple requests seeking an interview with Elizabeth Blanco, the superintendent.\par \par \uc2 In a March 26 guest editorial published in a district newsletter, Ms. Blanco wrote that she understood the desire to support students and teachers. ''However, we also face the reality that without cuts, we would face an even more severe fiscal situation next year,'' Ms. Blanco wrote.\par \par \uc2 By combining federal money, fire recovery funds and other adjustments, the \uc2 Pasadena Unified School District\uc2  was able to create 25 new positions for ''teacher on special assignment'' roles to help soften the blow of the layoffs. Ms. Blanco said that workers who have received layoff notices can apply for those positions.\par \par \uc2 By the end of the school year, many notices are often rescinded after districts confirm their budgets and assess staffing needs. As of Thursday, three layoff notices had been rescinded, according to the union.\par \par \uc2 The layoffs have shaken young teachers and those who are new to the district, including Consuelo Martinez, 27, who taught at Muir High for two years and received a layoff notice in March.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Martinez's home was not affected by the fire, but she has tried to support co-workers and students who lost homes or were displaced, setting up racks of donated clothes in her classroom.\par \par \uc2 ''After the fires, for a lot of them, coming back to school was like trying to get into normalcy and community and support,'' Ms. Martinez said of students. ''And now they see something being taken away from them that was helping them feel safe.''\par \par \uc2 The layoff notice wasn't a first for some teachers, including Mr. Lopez. He also received one last year. It was ultimately rescinded and he kept his job. But now, he said, it's harder to be optimistic that he'll be able to stay on, in the district where he grew up and went to school himself.\par \par \uc2 ''This year, it feels really different,'' Mr. Lopez said. ''There's way more uncertainty.''\par \par \uc2 He has been busy sorting out his plans -- where to live, and how to navigate insurance claims and whether he'll rebuild on the property.\par \par \uc2 ''It's been a lot to navigate and juggle,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Owens Ordonez, 27, who teaches biology and environmental science at Muir High, was told by the district last month he was being laid off. In the weeks since, his friends encouraged him to update his r\u233\'00\'E9sum\u233\'00\'E9 and start applying for jobs, but he said preparing for next steps was more difficult after he was displaced by the fire.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ordonez's home in Altadena survived the flames, but he has been unable to live there because of smoke damage.\par \par \uc2 Then he received a bit of good news last week: His layoff notice had been rescinded. He will keep his job for the next school year.\par \par \uc2 The news brought mixed emotions. ''I don't think I rejoiced,'' he said. ''I was just once again in shock. I felt kind of guilty.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250407el470004f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
