\par\par \uc2 CALCULATOR\par \uc2 Real Estate Desk; SECTRE\par \b \uc2 Carving Out New Living Spaces\b0\par\par\uc2 By Julia Echikson \par \uc2 333 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 April 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Municipalities are encouraging office conversions with incentives and rule changes.\par \par \uc2 Although Covid-19 has subsided and returned many employees to their cubicles, continued remote work and a housing shortage has spurred more office buildings to be converted to apartment buildings. Nearly 71,000 residences across the country are in the pipeline, more than triple the number three years ago, according to a report from RentCafe.\par \par \uc2 The report defined projects in the pipeline as those that have been approved, remain under construction or are in the planning phases. Researchers analyzed data from RentCafe's sister company, Yardi, which surveys commercial properties nationwide, to reach their conclusions.\par \par \uc2 New York, the country's largest office market and one of the most-competitive residential rental markets, counts the most conversions, 8,310. High-profile projects include the transformation of the former \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  headquarters in Midtown, set to deliver 1,600 apartments.\par \par \uc2 Washington D.C., came in second with 6,533 conversions, a number that could rise with the \uc2 Department of Government Efficiency\uc2 's quest to cut office leases.\par \par \uc2 Despite the boom, creating residences out of offices is a challenge, and not all buildings are well-suited. Properties from the early 20th century are best, thanks to narrow floor plans, which make it easier to design bedrooms with windows.\par \par \uc2 But to encourage conversions, municipalities are providing financial incentives and streamlined processes. San Francisco has updated building codes and is simplifying the approval process. Washington and New York provide tax abatements or exemptions, which in New York can be as much as 90 percent for buildings with at least 25 percent affordable apartments, according to the report.\par \par \uc2 Developers must also overcome high interest rates and the effect that tariffs could have on prices of materials, and the campaign to deport illegal immigrants, which could reduce the work force the construction industry has long relied on.\par \par \uc2 For weekly email updates on residential real estate news, sign up here.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250406el460003j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
