\par\par \uc2 Real Estate Desk; SECTRE\par \b \uc2 Building Homes Under the Trump Agenda\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ronda Kaysen and Tag Christof \par \uc2 1,506 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 April 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Take a line-by-line look at the estimated costs of building this four-bedroom home in Phoenix, Ariz., under President Trump's agenda.\par \par \uc2 Update April 4, 2025: This article has been updated to reflect news developments on tariffs.\par \par \uc2 Building a home in the United States is not cheap. This home in Phoenix, Ariz., will cost $1.3 million to build.\par \par \uc2 President Trump's immigration and tariff policies could make building such a house more expensive.\par \par \uc2 More immigrants work in construction than any other industry. Nearly one in four construction workers are noncitizen immigrants, but the numbers soar much higher in some trades.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump has already enacted a 25 percent global tariff on aluminum and steel; a 25 percent tariff on many goods from Canada and Mexico; and a 20 percent blanket tariff on Chinese goods, on top of a 10 percent tariff from his first term. This week he imposed a 10 percent tariff on nearly all imports, with additional levies on 57 countries.\par \par \uc2 While a builder can swap some goods for other ones, perhaps forgo Mexican tiles for locally-made ones, economists estimate that prices will rise and those costs will likely pass onto home buyers, who are already struggling to afford to buy in a market where both interest rates and prices are high.\par \par \uc2 A house is the sum of its parts. So how much more could each piece of this home cost to build with tariffs and without immigrant labor? And how much more could it cost you to buy a home like this one in the future?\par \par \uc2 This article has been updated to reflect news developments on tariffs.\par \par \uc2 Nathan Anderson, 40, who builds high-end homes in Phoenix, bought this small, older house on a quiet suburban street lined with palm trees, with a plan to tear it down and replace it with a larger, modern one.\par \par \uc2 He doesn't hire the crews or select the materials -- Mr. Anderson, the owner of Rebuild the Block, signs a contract with a trade partner who handles those details. But he keeps a detailed budget that accounts for his costs -- totaling about $1.29 million.\par \par \uc2 A shock to the labor market by a loss of workers through deportation and the fear of deportation could potentially drive up wages. And with the existing industry labor shortage, jobs could cost more and take longer to complete with fewer workers available.\par \par \uc2 This house is almost finished, so his budget is mostly locked in. But Mr. Anderson sat down with \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  before the latest round of tariffs were announced and estimated how his labor costs might change without immigrant workers, based on his personal experience, and using this house as a model. The Times also applied national and regional labor trends to his calculations, and analyzed construction industry work force demographics from census survey data. Given that Trump policies have affected both documented and undocumented immigrants, we did not differentiate between the two groups.\par \par \uc2 To examine the potential effects of tariffs on Mr. Anderson's budget, The Times turned to the Observatory of Economic Complexity, a tool that analyzes trade flows and tariff data. The analysis simulated the potential impact of a 25 percent increase on tariffs on goods from Canada and Mexico and a 10 percent increase on those from China. The more recent tariffs would drive the prices up even further, largely because China's new higher rate applies to key home-building imports like HVAC systems and tile.\par \par \uc2 Without immigrant workers, Mr. Anderson's labor costs could jump by 17 percent, adding $116,000 to his budget. The Observatory of Economic Complexity, using conservative calculations, estimated his material costs could rise by nearly 5 percent.\par \par \uc2 The house that Mr. Anderson is building was listed for sale in early March for $2.65 million. If building it had cost him another $236,000, Mr. Anderson said he would have listed the house for as much as $2.9 million. ''That's if the market will bear it,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Here is a glance at many of the major line items, and how they would have changed, with tariffs and without immigrant labor. Some figures have been rounded for brevity.\par \par \uc2 The Foundation\par \par \uc2 Last September, once the architectural drawings were complete, the building permits approved and the site prepared for construction, a crew arrived to lay the foundation, costing about $59,000. On the busiest days, as many as eight workers were on site. Some days, as few as three or four. Almost 70 percent, or $40,000, of the foundation budget, went to labor. About 90 percent of the crew were immigrants. Without them, his labor costs could swell to $49,800.\par \par \uc2 In 2023, the United States became the world's largest importer of cement, a key ingredient for concrete. Conservatively, tariffs could drive up the costs for the house construction by 18 percent.\par \par \uc2 The Framing\par \par \uc2 By the middle of October, with the foundation laid, the framing got underway. Framing is a labor intensive job, and about 75 percent of this crew were immigrants. About 62 percent of his $107,400 framing budget -- $67,000 -- was spent on labor. Without those workers, labor costs could cost Mr. Anderson another $16,000.\par \par \uc2 With tariffs, the lumber for the framing could add $1,760 to his budget.\par \par \uc2 The Windows and Doors\par \par \uc2 In January, the windows and doors arrived, costing $49,400. Of that, only $6,400 was spent on labor. Mr. Anderson said that changes to labor costs without immigrant workers ''would be minimal.''\par \par \uc2 The Roof and the Siding\par \par \uc2 By March, work began on the roof and siding, and 90 percent of the roofers were immigrants. (Nearly half of roofers in the United States are not citizens.) Without them, labor costs could rise by 30 percent.\par \par \uc2 Materials for stucco siding cost $15,000. With tariffs, that line item could cost Mr. Anderson another $1,000.\par \par \uc2 Plumbing, Electrical and Mechanicals\par \par \uc2 Almost 90 percent of electricians are U.S. citizens, and 81 percent of plumbers. Mr. Anderson's site was no exception. Mr. Anderson said his labor costs for electrical work, the HVAC systems and plumbing would be unlikely to change.\par \par \uc2 But his material costs could rise significantly, with tariffs. Electrical materials could increase by more than $4,800, while his HVAC system could cost him another $1,350.\par \par \uc2 The Insulation and Drywall\par \par \uc2 With the exterior completed, new crews arrived to add the insulation and drywall, labor-heavy tasks dominated by immigrant workers. Mr. Anderson spent 80 percent of his $45,000 insulation and drywall budget on labor.\par \par \uc2 A crew of two workers insulated the house, while eight to 10 workers hung the drywall. If Mr. Anderson had to replace all those workers, his labor costs could rise by 35 percent, a $12,570 increase.\par \par \uc2 Tariffs would increase material costs noticeably, too. In 2023, America imported more Gypsum, the material for drywall, than any other country, and so drywall is a material that could be particularly sensitive to tariffs. The price of drywall could rise by 22 percent, adding $1,540 to the $27,600 bill.\par \par \uc2 The Flooring\par \par \uc2 Flooring materials are the kinds of finishes that can persuade a buyer to make an offer. With tariffs, the costs for wood and tile flooring, many of which Mr. Anderson imported from China, could rise modestly.\par \par \uc2 But the crew laying the floors was almost entirely immigrant workers, and without them, Mr. Anderson anticipated that his costs could rise by 30 percent, to $51,000.\par \par \uc2 The Painting and Wallcovering\par \par \uc2 Painting and wallcoverings cost $46,000, with labor accounting for 80 percent of the cost. More than 47 percent of painters working in construction are not U.S. citizens, and immigrants made up nearly the entire crew for the Phoenix home. Mr. Anderson estimates his labor costs could rise by $7,400.\par \par \uc2 Tariffs could add 10.5 percent to the cost of house paint, raising the price from $8,400 to $9,300.\par \par \uc2 Cabinets and Countertops\par \par \uc2 The kitchen is the showpiece of any house, and the materials are expensive. Of the $96,000 spent on cabinets and countertops, 85 percent of that went to materials. Tariffs could drive those material costs up by more than $13,000.\par \par \uc2 However, cabinet and countertop installation relies heavily on immigrant labor, and the $19,000 labor costs could rise by 30 percent.\par \par \uc2 The Appliances\par \par \uc2 Mr. Anderson said it was unlikely that his $2,000 appliance installation costs would change with a loss of immigrant labor. However, the $24,000 he spent on materials could go up by $2,620, with tariffs.\par \par \uc2 Design, Management, Landscaping and Other Costs\par \par \uc2 ''The market is tough with interest rates right now,'' Mr. Anderson said.\par \par \uc2 But by this summer, Mr. Anderson hopes to attract a buyer for the new house with four bedrooms, oversize windows and a pool.\par \par \uc2 The asking price is $2.65 million. The next house he builds could be $2.9 million.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250406el460002w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
