\par\par \uc2 Money and Business/Financial Desk; SECTBU\par \b \uc2 30%\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kristen Bayrakdarian \par \uc2 524 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 March 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The rise in unemployment for college graduates since 2022.\par \par \uc2 What is the value of a college degree in today's job market?\par \par \uc2 Though the unemployment rate for college graduates is just 2.6 percent, that's a 30 percent increase from just two years ago -- compared with an 18 percent increase in the work force overall.\par \par \uc2 Unemployment has been highest for those with bachelor's degrees or some college but no degree, while joblessness has been steady or falling for those with advanced degrees or without a high school diploma, according to an analysis of recently released \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of New York\uc2  data by \uc2 Julia Pollak\uc2 , the chief economist for \uc2 ZipRecruiter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At the same time, hiring rates have slowed more for jobs that require a college degree than for any other type of job, ADP Research, a firm that studies the labor market, found.\par \par \uc2 ''We're seeing a meaningful transition in the way work is done in the white-collar world,'' said \uc2 Carl Tannenbaum\uc2 , the chief economist of \uc2 Northern Trust\uc2 . ''I tell people a wave is coming.''\par \par \uc2 Some economists say that these changes are minor enough that there is little cause for concern, and that by historical measures for all education groups, unemployment is low and the job market strong.\par \par \uc2 But there are other signs that a bachelor's degree doesn't hold the same weight it once did -- especially as technology becomes more advanced and has the ability to automate certain white-collar jobs.\par \par \uc2 Some economists argue that the flattening of the wage gap over the past 15 years between those with a college degree and those without -- though it remains wide -- could reflect both an overabundance of college graduates (as college attendance increases) as well as a decreased need for college degrees in an automated world.\par \par \uc2 For example, certain jobs, such as bookkeeping, were once attractive to college graduates but can now more or less be done by sophisticated information technology. The rise of powerful artificial intelligence models only adds to the pressure, as a variety of research has found that A.I. has the power to perform jobs that require higher levels of education, creativity and cognitive skills.\par \par \uc2 ''To be brutally honest, we had a hierarchy of things that technology could do, and we felt comfortable saying things like creative work, professional work, emotional intelligence would be hard for machines to ever do,'' said \uc2 Erik Brynjolfsson\uc2 , a professor at the Stanford Institute for Human-Centered A.I. ''Now that's all been upended.''\par \par \uc2 ''If shame was still a thing, this hearing would be shameful.''\par \par \uc2 Representative Stephen F. Lynch, Democrat of Massachusetts, at a House hearing on Wednesday where Republicans accused \uc2 PBS\uc2  and \uc2 NPR\uc2  of institutional bias.\par \par \uc2 ''For some people, having TikTok has literally been life saving.''\par \par \uc2 TikTok, writing on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , about a woman in kidney failure who was able to find a kidney transplant through the app.\par \par \uc2 ''The folks at the lower end of the buying pool are going to suffer the most.''\par \par \uc2 Erin Keating, executive analyst at \uc2 Cox Automotive\uc2 , on the potential effect of President Trump's automobile tariffs.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250330el3u00066\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
