\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECT\par \b \uc2 China Frees Employees of U.S. Consulting Firm After 2-Year Detention\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Pierson and Keith Bradsher \par \uc2 531 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 March 2025\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 The New York Times on the Web\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The release of the five employees, detained during a crackdown on foreign due diligence, comes as Beijing is trying to bring back overseas investment.\par \par \uc2 China has released five employees of an American corporate investigations firm, the \uc2 Mintz Group\uc2 , two years after they were detained as part of a crackdown by Beijing on foreign business consultancies.\par \par \uc2 ''We understand that the Mintz Group Beijing employees who were detained, all Chinese nationals, have now all been released,'' the firm said in a statement on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 ''We are grateful to the Chinese authorities that our former colleagues can now be home with their families,'' the company said.\par \par \uc2 The employees' release comes as China is trying to revive overseas investment to help revive its sluggish economy. Dozens of foreign executives, including \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2  of \uc2 Apple\uc2  and \uc2 Cristiano Amon\uc2 , the chief executive of \uc2 Qualcomm\uc2 , are visiting Beijing this week to attend a development forum. Foreign investment that slowed to a crawl during Covid has not recovered in the past couple years, as weak sales and overall demand in the Chinese economy have made it less attractive.\par \par \uc2 Several dozen chief executives and other leaders of global companies were expected to meet with China's top leader, Xi Jinping, on Friday at the Great Hall of the People in Beijing, according to a person with knowledge of the planning who spoke on the condition of anonymity because he was not authorized to comment. The gathering would be the third time in 17 months that Mr. Xi has met with the leaders of multinational companies, including in San Francisco in late 2023, after a summit with President \uc2 Joseph R. Biden Jr.\uc2  outside the city.\par \par \uc2 The business forum was also attended by Senator Steve Daines of Montana, who said in an interview with \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  that he was trying to lay the groundwork for a meeting between President Trump and Mr. Xi.\par \par \uc2 The Mintz employees, who have not been identified, were detained during a raid of the firm's Beijing office in March 2023. Later that year, China fined the company about $1.5 million for engaging in ''foreign-related statistical investigation activities without obtaining approval,'' according to Chinese state media.\par \par \uc2 Mintz, which is based in New York and has offices around the world, specializes in due diligence work -- background checks, asset tracing, and fraud and corruption investigations -- for client firms before they make investment decisions.\par \par \uc2 That kind of work became the target of Mr. Xi's government, which appears to view due diligence into Chinese supply chains and Chinese corporate activity as a threat to national security and the ruling Communist Party's grip on power.\par \par \uc2 A month after Mintz's office was raided, Chinese authorities visited the Shanghai offices of the U.S. management consulting firm \uc2 Bain & Company\uc2  and questioned its employees.\par \par \uc2 The crackdown on foreign consultancies was also viewed as a response to the Biden administration's introduction of export controls on advanced U.S. technology, such as semiconductors, to China.\par \par \uc2 Berry Wang contributed reporting from Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 Berry Wang contributed reporting from Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020250325el3p00057\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
